content.paragraph:
The diagonal entries in an $m \times n$ matrix $A = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      a_{ij}
      \end{bmatrix}$ are $a_{11},a_{22},a_{33},\text{… }$, and they form the main diagonal  of $A$. A diagonal matrix  is a square $n \times n$ matrix whose nondiagonal entries are zero. An example is the $n \times n$ identity matrix, $I_{n}$. An $m \times n$ matrix whose entries are all zero is a zero matrix and is written as $0$. The size of a zero matrix is usually clear from the context.

Thus multiplication by $\begin{bmatrix}
      {A\textbf{b}_{1}} & {A\textbf{b}_{2}} & \cdots & {A\textbf{b}_{p}}
      \end{bmatrix}$ transforms $\textbf{x}$ into $A(B\textbf{x})$. We have found the matrix we sought!

example.paragraph:
Compute $A B$, where $A = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      2 & 3 \\
      1 & {- 5}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and $B = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{rrr}
      4 & 3 & 6 \\
      1 & {- 2} & 3
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$.﻿

Write $B = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      \textbf{b}_{1} & \textbf{b}_{2} & \textbf{b}_{3}
      \end{bmatrix}$, and compute:

content.paragraph:
To verify this rule, let $B = \begin{bmatrix}
      \textbf{b}_{1} & \cdots & \textbf{b}_{p}
      \end{bmatrix}$. Column $j$ of $A B$ is $A\textbf{b}_{j}$, and we can compute $A\textbf{b}_{j}$ by the row–vector rule for computing $A\textbf{x}$ from Section 1.4. The $i\,\text{t}\text{h}$ entry in $A\textbf{b}_{j}$ is the sum of the products of corresponding entries from row $i$ of $A$ and the vector $\textbf{b}_{j}$, which is precisely the computation described in the rule for computing the $(i,j)\text{-entry}$ of $A B$.﻿

example.paragraph:
Let $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      5 & 1 \\
      3 & {- 2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and $B = \begin{bmatrix}
      2 & 0 \\
      4 & 3
      \end{bmatrix}$. Show that these matrices do not commute. That is, verify that $A B \neq B\mspace{-180mu} A$.﻿

Clearly the data is listed differently in the two matrices. Canada and the United states have different traditions for whether the month or day comes first when writing a date. For matrix $T$, the day is listed in the first row and the month is listed in the second row. For matrix $C$, the month is listed in the first row and the day is listed in the second row. In order to use this data, the first and second rows need to be swapped in one of the matrices. Reviewing the effects of matrix multiplication in Table 1 of Section 1.9, notice that the matrix $A = \begin{bmatrix}
      0 & 1 \\
      1 & 0
      \end{bmatrix}$ switches the $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ coordinates of any vector $x = \begin{bmatrix}
      x_{1} \\
      x_{2}
      \end{bmatrix}$ it is applied to and indeed

statement.paragraph:
Let $A = \begin{bmatrix}
      4 & {- 1} \\
      5 & {- 2}
      \end{bmatrix}$. Compute $3I_{2} - A$ and $(3I_{2})A$.﻿

$A = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      {- 1} & 2 \\
      5 & 4 \\
      2 & {- 3}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,$, $B = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      3 & {- 4} \\
      {- 2} & 1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$﻿

$A = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      4 & {- 2} \\
      {- 3} & 0 \\
      3 & 5
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,$, $B = \,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      1 & 3 \\
      4 & {- 1}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$﻿

Let $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      2 & {- 3} \\
      {- 4} & 6
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $B = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      8 & 4 \\
      5 & 5
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, and $C = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      5 & {- 2} \\
      3 & 1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Verify that $AB = AC$ and yet $B \neq C$.﻿

Let $\textbf{r}_{1},\ldots,\textbf{r}_{p}$ be vectors in $\mathbf{\mathbb{R}}^{n}$, and let $Q$ be an $m \times n$ matrix. Write the matrix $\begin{bmatrix}
      {Q\textbf{r}_{1}\cdots Q\textbf{r}_{p}}
      \end{bmatrix}$ as a product of two matrices (neither of which is an identity matrix).

(T/F) If $A$ and $B$ are $2 \times 2$ with columns $\textbf{a}_{1}$, $\textbf{a}_{2}$, and $\textbf{b}_{1}$, $\textbf{b}_{2}$, respectively, then $AB = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{ll}
      {\mathbf{a}_{1}\mathbf{b}_{1}} & {\mathbf{a}_{2}\mathbf{b}_{2}}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$.﻿

(T/F) If $A$ and $B$ are $3 \times 3$ and $B = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{lll}
      \mathbf{b}_{1} & \mathbf{b}_{2} & \mathbf{b}_{3}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, then $AB = \lbrack A\textbf{b}_{1} + A\textbf{b}_{2} + A\textbf{b}_{3}\rbrack$.﻿

Let $\textbf{u} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 2} \\
      3 \\
      {- 4}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and $\textbf{v} = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      a \\
      b \\
      c
      \end{bmatrix}\,$. Compute $\textbf{u}^{T}\textbf{v}$, $\textbf{v}^{T}\textbf{u}$, $\textbf{uv}^{T}$, and $\textbf{v}\textbf{u}^{T}$.﻿

[T] Describe in words what happens when you compute $A^{5}$, $A^{10}$, $A^{20}$, and $A^{30}$ for$$A = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      {1/6} & {1/2} & {1/3} \\
      {1/2} & {1/4} & {1/4} \\
      {1/3} & {1/4} & {5/12}
      \end{bmatrix}$$

[T] Use the matrix $A = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      0 & 1 \\
      1 & 0
      \end{bmatrix}$ to switch the first and second rows of the matrix $M$ containing dates of accidents at the Montreal Trudeau Airport.$$\begin{array}{l}
      \text{Montreal:} \\
      { M = \begin{bmatrix}
      2 & 3 & 16 & 24 & 25 & 26 & 6 & 7 & 19 & 26 \\
      1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 2 & 2 & 2 & 2
      \end{bmatrix}}
      \end{array}$$

[T] Use the matrix $B = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      1 & 0 & 0 \\
      0 & 1 & 0
      \end{bmatrix}$ to remove the last row from the matrix $N$ containing dates of accidents at the New York JFK Airport.$$\begin{array}{l}
      \text{New~York:} \\
      { N = \begin{bmatrix}
      1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\
      1 & 12 & 21 & 22 & 3 & 20 & 21 \\
      2020 & 2020 & 2020 & 2020 & 2020 & 2020 & 2020
      \end{bmatrix}}
      \end{array}$$
